  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM CDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nSynopsis:\nA slow-moving frontal boundary will waver across central \nIllinois through Sunday, resulting in periodic showers and \nthunderstorms. Cooler, fall-like conditions will then settle in \nfor the upcoming week. Although a drier weather pattern is \ngenerally expected, persistent low clouds and sporadic sprinkles\nare likely to continue throughout next week. The potential for \nsevere weather and flooding will stay low beyond Sunday.\n\n\nOvernight through Sunday Morning:\nThe most widespread shower and thunderstorm coverage will take \nshape overnight near the Interstate 80 corridor as robust \nshortwave energy rides over the surface boundary. Uncertainty \nremains regarding how far south of I-80 convection will reach, \nbut with the nose of the low-level jet positioned north of the \narea through Sunday morning, precipitation is unlikely to extend\nsignificantly south of a Macomb-to-Champaign line. Heavy \nlocalized rain exceeding 1 inch carries low confidence (20% or \nless). Shower activity will abate by midday Sunday as the low-\nlevel jet weakens and mid-level subsidence overspreads the \nregion behind the departing wave.\n\n\nSunday Afternoon:\nAnother shortwave impulse traversing the sagging front will \nlikely trigger renewed scattered thunderstorm development south \nof Interstate 72 on Sunday afternoon. Pooling dewpoints in the \nlower 70s along the boundary will yield around 1500 J/kg MLCAPE,\ncoinciding with an intensifying westerly flow regime that will \nboost bulk shear into the 30 kt range. Although mid-level lapse \nrates remain unimpressive, the overlapping CAPE and shear \nparameter space may support a few stronger updrafts capable of \nlocalized marginally severe wind and hail.\n\n\nSunday Night through Midweek:\nEnsemble and deterministic guidance remain in strong agreement\nthat a closed upper-level low will migrate eastward across the\nPlains, entering the Mid-Mississippi Valley by Monday. This\ndisturbance will support additional rain chances through Monday\nmorning before it dismantles the dominant heat ridge while\ndriving the frontal boundary south and east of our area. In its\nwake, expansive surface high pressure will establish itself \nbeneath a blocking split- flow regime, ushering in significantly\ncooler and drier conditions. Daytime highs Monday through \nWednesday are forecast to settle into the upper 60s to lower \n70s, and minor shortwave perturbations rotating around the \npersistent low overhead could maintain brief, isolated \nsprinkles.",
